"[2510.12029v1] CPR: Mitigating Large Language Model Hallucinations with Curative Prompt Refinement (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12029v1)\nciteturn2842view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.12029v1\",\"lineno\":38}); Total lines: 210\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite60†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite1†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org L5: \nL6: cite61†Image: arxiv logo > cite3†cs > arXiv:2510.12029v1\nL7: \nL8: cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite5†Advanced Search L9: \nL10:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite62†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2510.12029v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 14 Oct 2025]\nL33: # Title:CPR: Mitigating Large Language Model Hallucinations with Curative Prompt Refinement\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite9†Jung-Woo Shim , cite10†Yeong-Joon Ju , cite11†Ji-Hoon Park , cite12†Seong-Whan Lee L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled CPR: Mitigating Large Language Model Hallucinations with Curative Prompt Refinement, by Jung-Woo Shim and 3 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) highlight their fluency in generating responses to diverse prompts. However, these models sometimes generate plausible yet incorrect ``hallucinated\" facts, undermining trust. A frequent but often overlooked cause of such errors is the use of poorly structured or vague prompts by users, leading LLMs to base responses on assumed rather than actual intentions.\nL41: To mitigate hallucinations induced by these ill-formed prompts, we introduce Curative Prompt Refinement (CPR), a plug-and-play framework for curative prompt refinement that 1) cleans ill-formed prompts, and 2) generates additional informative task descriptions to align the intention of the user and the prompt using a fine-tuned small language model. When applied to language models, we discover that CPR significantly increases the quality of generation while also mitigating hallucination.\nL42: Empirical studies show that prompts with CPR applied achieves over a 90\\% win rate over the original prompts without any external knowledge.\nL43: Comments: 2024 IEEE International Conference on Systems, Man, and Cybernetics (SMC), 7 pages, 2 figures\nL44: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL45: Cite as: cite15†arXiv:2510.12029 [cs.CL]\nL46:   (or  cite16†arXiv:2510.12029v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL47:   cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.12029†doi.org L48: \nL49: Focus to learn more\nL50: \nL51:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL52: Journal reference: 2024 IEEE International Conference on Systems, Man, and Cybernetics (SMC), Kuching, Malaysia, 2024, pp. 1604-1609\nL53: Related DOI:  cite18†https://doi.org/10.1109/SMC54092.2024.10830938†doi.org L54: \nL55: Focus to learn more\nL56: \nL57:   DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL58: ## Submission history\nL59: \nL60: From: Jungwoo Shim [cite19†view email ]\nL61: [v1] Tue, 14 Oct 2025 00:27:46 UTC (224 KB)\nL62: \nL63: Full-text links:\nL64: ## Access Paper:\nL65: \nL66:  View a PDF of the paper titled CPR: Mitigating Large Language Model Hallucinations with Curative Prompt Refinement, by Jung-Woo Shim and 3 other authors\nL67: \nL68:   * cite13†View PDF L69:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L70:   * cite20†TeX Source  L71: \nL72: cite21† view license †creativecommons.org L73: \nL74: Current browse context:\nL75: \nL76: cs.CL\nL77: \nL78: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L79: \nL80: cite24†new |  cite25†recent | cite26†2025-10 L81: \nL82: Change to browse by:\nL83: \nL84: cite27†cs L85: cite28†cs.AI L86: cite29†cs.LG L87: ### References & Citations\nL88: \nL89:   * cite30†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L90:   * cite31†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L91:   * cite32†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L92: \nL93: export BibTeX citation Loading...\nL94: \nL95: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL96: \nL97: ×\nL98: \nL99: loading...\nL100: \nL101: Data provided by:\nL102: \nL103: ### Bookmark\nL104: \nL105: cite33† †www.bibsonomy.org cite34† †reddit.com L106: \nL107: Bibliographic Tools\nL108: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL109: \nL110: Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL111: \nL112: Bibliographic Explorer (cite35†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL113: \nL114: Connected Papers Toggle\nL115: \nL116: Connected Papers (cite36†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL117: \nL118: Litmaps Toggle\nL119: \nL120: Litmaps (cite37†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL121: \nL122: scite.ai Toggle\nL123: \nL124: scite Smart Citations (cite38†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL125: \nL126: Code, Data, Media\nL127: # Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article\nL128: \nL129: alphaXiv Toggle\nL130: \nL131: alphaXiv (cite39†What is alphaXiv?†alphaxiv.org )\nL132: \nL133: Links to Code Toggle\nL134: \nL135: CatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers (cite40†What is CatalyzeX?†www.catalyzex.com )\nL136: \nL137: DagsHub Toggle\nL138: \nL139: DagsHub (cite41†What is DagsHub?†dagshub.com )\nL140: \nL141: GotitPub Toggle\nL142: \nL143: Gotit.pub (cite42†What is GotitPub?†gotit.pub )\nL144: \nL145: Huggingface Toggle\nL146: \nL147: Hugging Face (cite43†What is Huggingface?†huggingface.co )\nL148: \nL149: Links to Code Toggle\nL150: \nL151: Papers with Code (cite44†What is Papers with Code?†paperswithcode.com )\nL152: ScienceCast Toggle\nL153: \nL154: ScienceCast (cite45†What is ScienceCast?†sciencecast.org )\nL155: \nL156: Demos\nL157: # Demos\nL158: \nL159: Replicate Toggle\nL160: \nL161: Replicate (cite46†What is Replicate?†replicate.com )\nL162: \nL163: Spaces Toggle\nL164: \nL165: Hugging Face Spaces (cite47†What is Spaces?†huggingface.co )\nL166: \nL167: Spaces Toggle\nL168: \nL169: TXYZ.AI (cite48†What is TXYZ.AI?†txyz.ai )\nL170: \nL171: Related Papers\nL172: \nL173: # Recommenders and Search Tools\nL174: \nL175: Link to Influence Flower\nL176: \nL177: Influence Flower (cite49†What are Influence Flowers?†influencemap.cmlab.dev )\nL178: \nL179: Core recommender toggle\nL180: \nL181: CORE Recommender (cite50†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMulti-stage Prompt Refinement for Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.12032v1)\nciteturn2842view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.12032v1\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 736\nL70@P2: specific tasks [15] due to its emergent abilities obtained through knowledge distilla\u0002tion [16]. Although they lack the extensive data and capacity of LLMs, SLMs excel\nL71@P2-3: 3when fine-tuned for narrow, task-specific applications [17, 18]. Their computational\nL72@P3: efficiency makes them valuable in resource-constrained scenarios or when full LLM\nL73@P3: capabilities aren’t needed. SLMs are particularly suited for real-time applications or\nL74@P3: environments with limited computing resources [19, 20]. They perform well on smaller\nL75@P3: datasets, making them ideal for tasks like prompt refinement, where the focus is on im\u0002proving input quality before processing by larger models [21, 22]. Research shows that\nL76@P3: SLMs fine-tuned for tasks like error correction or semantic analysis can boost LLM\nL77@P3: performance while avoiding the high computational costs of LLMs [23]. Their effi\u0002ciency and adaptability make SLMs ideal for targeted applications that enhance LLM\nL78@P3: output accuracy.\nL79@P3: 2.3. Prompt Refinement\nL80@P3: Prompt refinement has become essential for improving input quality and reducing\nL81@P3: hallucinations in LLM outputs. Traditional strategies, such as using LLMs to rephrase\nL82@P3: or enhance prompts [24], often compromise computational efficiency. Techniques like\nL83@P3: reinforcement learning [25, 8] and human intervention [26, 27] have been used to\nL84@P3: iteratively improve prompt clarity, ensuring more accurate inputs for LLMs. Auto\u0002mated methods, including paraphrasing models and supplementary task descriptions,\nL85@P3: also help optimize prompts, improving output alignment with user intent [28]. How\u0002ever, these approaches typically rely on large models and high computational costs.\nL86@P3: In contrast, MPR leverages smaller, task-specific models to refine prompts efficiently,\nL87@P3: correcting common issues like punctuation, typographical errors, and misuse of key\nL88@P3: terms. This lightweight approach enhances prompt quality across LLM architectures\nL89@P3: without requiring significant computational resources.\nL90@P3: 3. Multi-stage Prompt Refinement\nL91@P3: 3.1. Dataset Construction for Fine-tuning\nL92@P3: A critical requirement for training the SLMs in MPR is the construction of a well\u0002curated and diverse dataset that addresses the various aspects of prompt refinement.\nL93@P3: The dataset is built from four primary sources: the Online Language Modeling (OLM)\nL94@P3-4: 4LLM\nL95@P4: Under favorable weather\nL96@P4: conditions, it is possible to see the\nL97@P4: coastline of Spain from certain\nL98@P4: points across the Gibraltar in\nL99@P4: Morocco.\nL100@P4: Ill-form detection SLM\nL101@P4: See from spaiin moroco? Prompt cleaning\nL102@P4: Stage 2: Cleaning prompts with specialized SLMs\nL103@P4: Stage 1: Detecting ill-formedness in prompts from non-expert users Stage 3: Iterative description generation process\nL104@P4: Cleaned prompt\nL105@P4: Keyword\nL106@P4: correction\nL107@P4: SLM\nL108@P4: Ambiguity\nL109@P4: correction\nL110@P4: SLM\nL111@P4: See from spaiin moroco?\nL112@P4: See from spaiin moroco?\nL113@P4: See from spaiin moroco?\nL114@P4: Prompt\nL115@P4: integration\nL116@P4: SLM\nL117@P4: Typographical\nL118@P4: correction\nL119@P4: SLM\nL120@P4: Prompt cleaning pipeline\nL121@P4: Ill-formed user prompt Cleaned prompt\nL122@P4: Is Spain visible from Morocco?\nL123@P4: See from spaiin moroco?\nL124@P4: Ill-formed user prompt\nL125@P4: Non-expert user\nL126@P4: Description\nL127@P4: merging\nL128@P4: SLM\nL129@P4: Description history\nL130@P4: Multi-stage description\nL131@P4: generating process\nL132@P4: Description\nL133@P4: SLM\nL134@P4: Generation\nL135@P4: Validation Description 𝑁\nL136@P4: If 1st_generation or\nL137@P4: none-valid description\nL138@P4: Collect all\nL139@P4: Is it valid?\nL140@P4: Yes or No\nL141@P4: Integrated\nL142@P4: descriptions\nL143@P4: Final prompt\nL144@P4: Description #1 …\nL145@P4: Description #2 …\nL146@P4: Final prompt\nL147@P4: Final prompt\nL148@P4: Required correction\nL149@P4: 1. Typographical\nL150@P4: 2. Keyword\nL151@P4: 3. Ambiguity\nL152@P4: Figure 1: Overview of the MPR framework. MPR is designed to systematically refine user prompts before\nL153@P4: they are processed by LLMs. The framework addresses common errors in prompts such as punctuation\nL154@P4: mistakes, typographical issues, and misuse of key terms that can lead to inaccurate or suboptimal outputs\nL155@P4: from LLMs.\nL156@P4: Wikipedia dataset [29], the Grammarly CoEdIT dataset (CoEdIT) [30] ,the Multi\u0002domain Question Rewriting dataset (MQR) [31], and the Magpie-Pro-300K-Filtered\nL157@P4: dataset (Magpie) [32]. Each dataset serves a unique purpose in training the model for\nL158@P4: tasks such as punctuation correction, grammar correction, paraphrasing, and descrip\u0002tion generation.\nL159@P4: The OLM Wikipedia dataset, sourced from high-quality English Wikipedia text, is\nL160@P4: ideal for fine-tuning SLMs on punctuation correction and grammar refinement. Using\nL161@P4: GPT-3.5-turbo, we curated 10,000 grammatically perfect entries, serving as the gold\nL162@P4: standard in our training pipeline. To aid learning, we introduced systematic punctua\u0002tion errors, creating a paired dataset: one version with mistakes and the other as the\nL163@P4: corrected reference.\nL164@P4: The CoEdIT dataset, drawn from high-quality English text, focuses on non-meaning\u0002changing edits such as fluency, coherence, and style, making it ideal for training mod\u0002els in grammatical and stylistic refinement. Unlike typical datasets, CoEdIT uses\nL165@P4: instruction-based prompts to guide edits like grammar correction and paraphrasing.\nL166@P4: We developed 10,000 grammatically accurate entries as the gold standard for well\u0002formed text. To enhance error correction capabilities, we created a parallel dataset\nL167@P4-5: 5with deliberate grammatical mistakes paired with corrected versions.\nL168@P5: The MQR dataset contains 2,114 pairs of paraphrased questions across various do\u0002mains, making it a key resource for training models to handle nuanced linguistic trans\u0002formations beyond simple synonym swaps. It helps models recognize semantic equiv\u0002alence between different sentence structures while preserving meaning. For example,\nL169@P5: “What is the capital of France?” might be paraphrased as “Could you tell me which\nL170@P5: city serves as the capital of France?”\nL171@P5: The Magpie Dataset, consisting of 300,000 keyword-description pairs, trains models\nL172@P5: to generate concise, accurate descriptions for specific terms, including technical jargon\nL173@P5: and abbreviations. It helps models interpret complex terminology and produce clear\nL174@P5: explanations. For instance, when given “ViT\" (Vision Transformer), the model should\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSelf-Verifying ReflectionHelps Transformers with CoT Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12157v1)\nciteturn2842view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.12157v1\",\"lineno\":180}); Total lines: 1695\nL167: Following the RLVR paradigm [cite79†15 ], we use binary outcome rewards (i.e., $1$ for correct answers and $0$ otherwise) computed by a rule-based answer checker $\\operatorname{ORM}(Q,A)$. When training the self-verifying policy $\\tilde{\\pi}$, the RMTP treats verification ${V}_{t}$ as a part of the augmented step ${\\tilde{R}}_{t}$, simulating R1-like training [cite77†5 ] where reflection and solution planning are jointly optimized. We mainly use GRPO [cite92†31 ] as the algorithms to optimize policies.\nL168: Details of RL fine-tuning are elaborated in Appendix cite28†B .\nL169: ## 4 Theoretical results\nL170: This section establishes theoretical conditions under which self-verifying reflection (RMTP or RTBS in Section cite15†3.2 ) enhances reasoning accuracy (the probability of deriving correct answers). The general relationship between the verification ability and reasoning accuracy (discussed in Appendix cite33†C.1 ) for any MTP is intractable as the states and transitions can be arbitrarily specified.\nL171: Therefore, to derive interpretable insights, we discuss a simplified prototype of reasoning that epitomizes the representative principle of CoTs — to incrementally express complex relations by chaining the local relation in each step [cite85†25 ]. Specifically, Given query $Q$ as the initial state, we view a CoT as the step-by-step process that reduces the complexity within states:\nL172:   * •\nL173: \nL174: We define $\\mathcal{S}_{n}$ as the set of states with a complexity scale of $n$. For simplicity, we assume that each step, if not rejected by reflection, reduces the complexity scale by $1$. Therefore, the scale $n$ is the number of effective steps required to derive an answer.\nL175: \nL176:   * •\nL177: An answer $A$ is a state with a scale of $0$, i.e. $A\\in\\mathcal{S}_{0}$. Given an input query $Q$, the answers $\\mathcal{S}_{0}$ are divided into positive (correct) answers $\\mathcal{S}_{0}^{+}$ and negative (wrong) answers $\\mathcal{S}_{0}^{-}$.\nL178: \nL179:   * •\nL180: \nL181: States $\\mathcal{S}_{n}$ ($n$ > 0) are divided into 1) positive states $\\mathcal{S}_{n}^{+}$ that potentially lead to correct answers and 2) negative states $\\mathcal{S}_{n}^{-}$ leading to only incorrect answers through forward transitions.\nL182: Consider a self-verifying policy $\\tilde{\\pi}=\\{\\pi,\\mathcal{V}\\}$ to solve this simplified task. We describe its fundamental abilities using the following probabilities (whose meanings will be explained afterwards):\nL183:  | $$\\displaystyle\\mu:=p_{{R}\\sim\\pi}(\\mathcal{T}({S},{R})\\in\\mathcal{S}^{+}_{n-1}\\mid{S}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{n}^{+})$$  |  | (3)\nL184:  | $$\\displaystyle e_{+}:=p_{{R},{V}\\sim\\tilde{\\pi}}(\\mathcal{T}({S},{R})\\in\\mathcal{S}^{-}_{n-1},\\text{``$\\times$''}\\notin{V}\\mid{S}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{n}^{+}),$$  |  | (4)\nL185:  | $$\\displaystyle e_{-}:=p_{{R},{V}\\sim\\tilde{\\pi}}(\\mathcal{T}({S},{R})\\in\\mathcal{S}^{+}_{n-1},\\text{``$\\times$''}\\in{V}\\mid{S}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{n}^{+}),$$  |  | (5)\nL186:  | $$\\displaystyle f:=p_{{R},{V}\\sim\\tilde{\\pi}}(\\text{``$\\times$''}\\in{V}\\mid{S}\\in\\mathcal{S}_{n}^{-}).$$  |  | (6)\nL187: To elaborate, $\\mu$ measures the planning ability, defined as the probability that $\\pi$ plans a step that leads to a positive next state, given that the current state is positive. For verification abilities, we measure the rates of two types of errors: $e_{+}$ (false positive rate) is the probability of accepting a step that leads to a negative state, and $e_{-}$ (false negative rate) is the probability of rejecting a step that leads to a positive state.\nL188: Additionally, $f$ is the probability of rejecting any step on negative states, providing the chance of tracing back to previous states. Given these factors, Figure cite116†5 illustrates the state transitions in non-reflective (vanilla MTP) and reflective (RMTB and RTBS) reasoning.\nL189: (a) Non-reflective reasoning\nL190: \nL191: (b) Reflective reasoning through an RMTP or RTBS\nL192: \nL193: Figure 5: The diagram of state transitions starting from scale $n$ in the simplified reasoning, where probabilities are attached to solid lines. In (b) reflective reasoning, the dashed-line arrow presents the trace-back move after $m$ attempts in RTBS.\nL194: For input problems with scale $n$, we use $\\rho(n)$, $\\tilde{\\rho}(n)$, and $\\tilde{\\rho}_{m}(n)$ to respectively denote the reasoning accuracy using no reflection, RMTP, and RTBS (with width $m$). Obviously, we have $\\rho(n)=\\mu^{n}$. In contrast, the mathematical forms of $\\tilde{\\rho}(n)$ and $\\tilde{\\rho}_{m}(n)$ are more complicated and therefore left to Appendix cite36†C.2 .\nL195: Our main result provides simple conditions for the above factors $(\\mu,e_{-},e_{+},f)$ to ensure an improved accuracy when reasoning through an RMTP or RTBS.\nL196: ###### Theorem 1.\nL197: \nL198: In the above simplified problem, consider a self-verifying policy $\\tilde{\\pi}$ where $\\mu$, $e_{-}$, and $e_{+}$ are non-trivial (i.e. neither $0$ nor $1$). Let $\\alpha:=\\mu e_{-}+(1-\\mu)(1-e_{+})$ denote the rejection probability on positive states. Given an infinite computation budget, for $n>0$ we have:\nL199: \nL200:   * •\nL201: $\\tilde{\\rho}(n)\\geq\\rho(n)$ if and only if $e_{-}+e_{+}\\leq 1$, where equalities hold simultaneously; furthermore, reducing either $e_{-}$ or $e_{+}$ strictly increases $\\tilde{\\rho}(n)$.\nL202: \nL203:   * •\nL204: \nL205: $\\tilde{\\rho}_{m}(n)>\\tilde{\\rho}(n)$ for a sufficiently large $n$ if and only if $f>\\alpha$ and $m>\\frac{1}{1-\\alpha}$; furthermore, such a gap of $\\tilde{\\rho}_{m}(n)$ over $\\tilde{\\rho}(n)$ increases strictly with $f$.\nL206: Does reflection require a strong verifier? Theorem cite117†1 shows that RMTP improves performance over vanilla MTP if the verification errors $e_{+}$ and $e_{-}$ are properly bounded, which does not necessitate a strong verifier. In our simplified setting, this only requires the verifier $\\mathcal{V}$ to be better than random guessing (which ensures $e_{-}+e_{+}=1$). This also indicates a trivial guarantee of RTBS, as an infinitely large width ($m\\to+\\infty$) substantially converts RTBS to RMTB.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnalyzing and Internalizing Complex Policy Documents for LLM Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11588v1)\nciteturn2842view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11588v1\",\"lineno\":165}); Total lines: 523\nL155: To support this, we curate large volumes of scenario-simulation data that go beyond memorization, emphasizing the practical application of policy rules and enabling the model to fully exercise its reasoning capabilities. Unlike standard CPT data focused on rote recall, this simulation data operationalizes the policy document, transforming abstract rules into executable workflows. An intuitive explanation of why such data better facilitate model learning is provided in Appendix cite39†F .\nL156: During this process, the LLM synthesizes scenarios and samples concrete instances from the environment database. For example, given the complex policy specification in Figure cite57†1 , the LLM can generate numerous queries by sampling user and reservation details, then compute the correct number of non-free checked bags and the corresponding total fee. Finally, we incorporate SFT trajectory data as an auxiliary source to better prepare the model for downstream task solving.\nL157: Although all curated data are structured in QA format, they are employed within a continued d pretraining (CPT) paradigm, where the objective is to minimize the standard language modeling loss $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{CPT}}=-\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\log P_{\\theta}(x_{t}\\mid x_{<t})$, with $\\theta$ denoting model parameters and $x_{t}$ the target token at position $t$. The CPT stage enhances the model’s ability to internalize and reason over policy content, rather than merely memorizing query answer pairs.\nL158: We validate the effectiveness of our curated data and training objective in Section § cite16†4 .\nL159: ## 4 Evaluation of Policy Document Internalization\nL160: \nL161: ### 4.1 Experiment Settings\nL162: #### Model and Data Settings\nL163: We use Qwen-2.5-32B and Qwen-3-32B for policy document internalization, chosen for their strong prior knowledge and distinct performance when complex policy documents are provided in context. To evaluate complexity effects, we sample datasets that control other dimensions while varying workflow complexity from level (1) to (3), as well as datasets that vary task-level complexity with level (3), (5), (8), and (12) tasks. For SFT, we provide between 1K, 5K, and up to 30K training samples.\nL164: We also apply our approach to $\\tau$-Bench, which offers only 500 training samples with no CoT based reasoning. Using Qwen-3-32B, we self-generate CoT trajectories and yield 282 SFT samples. More details are in Appendix cite36†D .\nL165: #### Evaluation Framework and Metrics\nL166: The primary focus of our evaluation is task completion after policy internalization, where agents must follow the internalized policy document to execute predefined tasks. To provide a more comprehensive assessment, we also consider scenarios involving policy substitution or override, policy-referral QA grounded in the document, and general instruction-following tests using IFeval (cite55†Zhou et al., 2023 ). Detailed settings are in Appendix cite38†E .\nL167: Task completion is measured by success rate (SR), policy QAs are scored on a 0–5 scale by a language model and rescaled to 0–100, and instruction following is evaluated by average accuracy.\nL168: Table 3: Task-completion performance after policy internalization under varying workflow complexities, with SFT trajectory sizes from 1K to 30K. Our CAP-CPT + SFT consistently outperforms strong baselines, alleviates data sparsity, and reduces the gap between high- and low-complexity scenarios. On Qwen-2.5-32B, it even surpasses agent performance with the full policy in context.\nL169: Model  | Complexity  | Prompting  | Internalization Approach  | Internalization Training Data Size\nL170: 1K  | 5K  | 10K  | 20K  | 30K\nL171: Qwen2.5-32B  | Task (5) Workflow (1)  | 0.07  | Gold CoT SFT  | 0.04  | 0.80  | 0.95  | 0.97  | 0.98\nL172: CAP-CPT + Gold CoT SFT  | 0.57  | 0.94  | 0.98  | 0.98  | 0.99\nL173: Task (5) Workflow (2)  | 0.04  | Gold CoT SFT  | 0.03  | 0.23  | 0.31  | 0.47  | 0.59\nL174: CAP-CPT + Gold CoT SFT  | 0.43  | 0.66  | 0.74  | 0.88  | 0.90\nL175: Task (5) Workflow (3)  | 0.01  | Gold CoT SFT  | 0.00  | 0.14  | 0.26  | 0.32  | 0.52\nL176: CAP-CPT + Gold CoT SFT  | 0.36  | 0.63  | 0.72  | 0.85  | 0.85\nL177: Qwen3-32B  | Task (5) Workflow (1)  | 0.82  | Gold CoT SFT  | 0.03  | 0.41  | 0.55  | 0.71  | 0.78\nL178: CAP-CPT + Gold CoT SFT  | 0.44  | 0.67  | 0.72  | 0.74  | 0.80\nL179: Task (5) Workflow (2)  | 0.62  | Gold CoT SFT  | 0.02  | 0.18  | 0.23  | 0.35  | 0.42\nL180: CAP-CPT + Gold CoT SFT  | 0.27  | 0.35  | 0.46  | 0.53  | 0.57\nL181: Task (5) Workflow (3)  | 0.53  | Gold CoT SFT  | 0.01  | 0.13  | 0.17  | 0.31  | 0.36\nL182: CAP-CPT + Gold CoT SFT  | 0.16  | 0.27  | 0.39  | 0.41  | 0.47\nL183: cite77†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Performance curves for internalizing policy documents with varying workflow complexities on Qwen-2.5-32B, comparing the baseline with our method. Our approach consistently outperforms the baseline across all settings and substantially narrows the performance gap in high-complexity and data-sparse scenarios.\nL184: ### 4.2 Main results\nL185: #### CAP-CPT Significantly Boosts Performance\nL186: We evaluate agent task-completion performance under varying workflow complexities in Table cite78†3 , with corresponding performance curves in Figure cite79†7 . Relying solely on Gold CoT–enhanced trajectory data for SFT is highly data-intensive and results in large disparities across complexity levels. In contrast, our CAP-CPT approach consistently improves performance across all data splits, with particularly strong gains under data-sparse conditions.\nL187: Although the curated data is not explicitly optimized for task completion, it substantially strengthens policy internalization and narrows performance gaps: CAP-CPT reduces the disparity between high- and low-complexity scenarios by 37% on Qwen-2.5-32B and 21% on Qwen-3-32B, even with abundant SFT data. This yields more robust and generalizable policy understanding. Similar trends are observed under varying task-level complexities (Appendix cite36†D ).\nL188: Overall, our internalization achieves input token compression of up to 97.3%. Notably, internalization training on the strongest base models does not yield gains over the prompting baseline or over training on originally weaker models. We analyze this in Appendix cite39†F .\nL189: #### CAP-CPT Helps Under Broader Evaluation Settings\nL190: We evaluate agent post-internalization performance on policy-referral, policy-substitute, and policy-override tasks, as well as general instruction following. Results on Qwen-3-32B are shown in Table cite80†4 , with more comprehensive results in Appendix cite36†D . Across all policy-related tasks, our method substantially outperforms SFT baselines but does not surpass the prompting baseline, indicating that these out-of-domain tasks remain challenging and warrant further study.\n"
